00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.920
off when you sign up using&nbsp;
our link in the description.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.640
This algae is stressed.
And we can tell it’s stressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.160
because it’s doing something that you&nbsp;
might also do when you find yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.240
in a difficult situation. It’s turning red.
But while blushing brightly for everyone to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.680
might make your situation worse, this&nbsp;
algae can do something else that sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.920
honestly, very appealing: it can curl up in&nbsp;
a ball and just wait out the worst of it.&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.880
This algae is called Haematococcus, and&nbsp;
it is not, physiologically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.920
turning red through the same mechanisms that&nbsp;
you and I do when we are stressed and blushing.&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:10.240
We flush with blood. Meanwhile, the Haematococcus&nbsp;
does not have blood. But depending on the state of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.760
your ancient Greek, you might recognize its&nbsp;
name translates loosely to “blood berries,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.160
a name that seems fitting for a&nbsp;
round, reddish organism like this one.&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:27.200
But as poetically sanguinary as the name is,&nbsp;
Haematococcus flushes with something else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.960
altogether, a chemical we can see traveling&nbsp;
through the algae and up the food chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:39.280
until it winds up on our own plates.
But before we get to the dinner table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:47.280
let us talk about where we got our Haematococcus:&nbsp;
from a centuries old cemetery in Warsaw, Poland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:54.080
filled with beautiful sculptures and birdbaths.
For James, our master of microscopes, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.160
important to respect the tombs there. So, don’t&nbsp;
worry, these microbes don’t have particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:07.840
spooky origins. He’s there for the birdbaths,&nbsp;
where the algae lines the surfaces like rust.&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.960
The Haematococcus likely arrived at the&nbsp;
birdbath thanks to the birds themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.160
and there it would have remained until maybe&nbsp;
another bird came along, except this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.520
your favorite microbe hunter came first.
In addition to birdbaths, Haematococcus are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.360
often found in transient pools of freshwater,&nbsp;
like puddles after a rainfall. And as you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.280
see on your screen, not all Haematococcus are&nbsp;
red. Plenty of them are green, which makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.760
because Haematococcus is a member of the phylum&nbsp;
Chlorophyta and that phylum is full of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:48.960
other unicellular green algae like the volvox.
And as algae, Haematococcus are green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.920
photosynthetic, and very easy to eat, which makes&nbsp;
them a simple solid foundation for an ecosystem.&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.040
But even seemingly simple creatures&nbsp;
have requests of the world around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.240
whether that’s for certain levels of light, or a&nbsp;
particular temperature. And if you were to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.800
go to a hotel and your room was 100 degrees,&nbsp;
you got a few solid options to fix that problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.960
You could find the thermostat. And if that&nbsp;
doesn’t work, you could complain to the manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.400
or even just go find another place to stay.
But those are not great options for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.440
Haematococcus. There’s no thermostat to&nbsp;
change, there’s no manager and if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.120
temperature gets too high, there’s only so&nbsp;
much a single-celled body can do to survive.&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.760
So instead, the Haematococcus does what I’m&nbsp;
sure many of us wish we could do when we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.800
in an uncomfortable situation: it takes&nbsp;
a little bit of a break, forming a cyst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.720
that allows it to rest and survive&nbsp;
until conditions are good again.&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.320
When you’re looking at this mixture of&nbsp;
Haematococcus, you’re looking at a gathering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.240
of algae in different phases of their lives.&nbsp;
The most active ones are called macrozooids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:05.040
or zoospores. They might be shaped like spheres,&nbsp;
ellipsoids, or even pears. And they’re encased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.680
in a thick gelatinous wall, with two flagella&nbsp;
extending out in front to zip the algae around.&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.200
But most importantly, here at&nbsp;
this stage, the algae is green.&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.800
The Haematococcus rarely reproduces sexually,&nbsp;
and when it’s a macrozooid, it divides rapidly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.880
forming around 2 to 32 copies of itself. But&nbsp;
it makes sense to move around and make more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.760
yourself when conditions are good. What happens&nbsp;
though when you’re in a comfortable pool of water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:40.160
one day, and in much more dire straits the next?
Well, for the Haematococcus, this is the point at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.360
which it all begins to change. The algae&nbsp;
sheds its flagella, layers up its wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:52.240
and shifts into a ball. This is the&nbsp;
Haematococcus as a palmella, a resting cell.&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.920
But if stress has taught me anything,&nbsp;
it is that things can always get worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:03.600
And for this little cell, it can get hotter or&nbsp;
saltier or just too bright. And when that happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.000
the palmella morphs into an aplanospore.&nbsp;
Its walls become even heavier duty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.280
shielding it from the outside. But most striking,&nbsp;
this is when the haematococcus becomes red.&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.920
The red is practical, the product&nbsp;
of a chemical called astaxanthin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.240
Astaxanthin is a carotenoid, a type of reddish&nbsp;
yellow pigment found in plants and microbes. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:32.400
it’s more than just a pretty color. Astaxanthin&nbsp;
is an extremely powerful antioxidant, protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.240
the cyst from potential damage as it rests.
You’ve probably seen astaxanthin in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.560
except you might not have&nbsp;
called it that. You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:47.440
have just called it “pink,” or “salmon pink.”
Astaxanthin is found in plants, fungi, bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.560
and yes, in algae. And haematococcus, is&nbsp;
considered to be one of the best natural producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:59.600
of astaxanthin, able to produce up to 5% of its&nbsp;
dry weight in astaxanthin. For comparison, another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:08.080
astaxanthin-producing algae called Chlorella makes&nbsp;
around 0.001% of its dry weight in astaxanthin&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.560
Now the reason we find astaxanthin&nbsp;
in so many animals is because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.320
algae make for good food, whether you&nbsp;
are a shrimp, salmon, or flamingo.&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.880
It’s striking that the color we call “salmon&nbsp;
pink” is a sign of both the salmon’s contentment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:31.120
and the algae’s demise. Perhaps we should start&nbsp;
calling it “haematococcus pink” instead, an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:38.160
acknowledgement of the work of the haematococcus,&nbsp;
which endures so long after its death.&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.280
It would be a fitting tribute after all to a&nbsp;
color you might have eaten a few times yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.400
The salmon, sure, which might have fed on&nbsp;
synthetic astaxanthin or algae. But even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.640
the chicken eggs you buy might have gotten their&nbsp;
yolk color boosted by algae added to chicken feed.&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.680
Astaxanthin has its uses outside of our food,&nbsp;
whether in our cosmetics or nutraceuticals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.640
And while much of that is synthetic, a copy we’ve&nbsp;
learned to make by studying the chemistry of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:12.720
astaxanthin, there’s also a push in the market for&nbsp;
“natural” products, a push that has led to renewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:19.040
interest in the haematococcus algae and its&nbsp;
wondrous ability to produce its own astaxanthin.&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.880
But for the haematococcus that’s just something&nbsp;
weird going on in the outside world, a factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:32.080
far beyond its control and its interest. Whatever&nbsp;
comes its way--whether it's a scientist, a bird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.720
or a fish--all the haematococcus is concerned with&nbsp;
is its own survival. What belongs to the algae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.960
will one day belong to another organism, passed&nbsp;
on through chemistry and made into something new&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:53.120
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

